AI Problem: Fraud detection 

# What are the features?
Features:
- IP Address (if its available), Device info (Any kind like location, type, ect...)
- Transaction time, Transction type, and Amount
- The account number that sent and the account number that recieved

# Identify the information that the model could use as inputs.
What is the label?
- It can label them as binary numbers. For example, it can label fraud transactions as "1" and legit transactions as "0".

# What outcome should the model learn to predict?
What would training data contain?
It can contain a history of all transactions (including both current and previous ones), all the input features, and the label showing which is fraud and which is legit.

Why should testing data be kept separate?
It should be kept seperate to see how well the model detects fraud from new datasets. If you just test it on the old data set, it might just learn the accounts the scam and not the ways they use.

What could be a false positive?
Detecting a legit transaction as fraud

What could be a false negative?
Detecting a fraud transcation as legit

Would precision or recall be more important?
Explain your reasoning.
Id say recall is more important becuase if it detects a transaction as fraud while its legit, it wouldnt really hurt anyone (It would just be an inconvenience to the customer). But if it detects a fraud transaction as legit, then both the customer and business are hurt financially and maybe even emotionally as losing money is very stressful. It just better to detect a legit transaction as fraud and cause a minor inconveniance then detecting fraud as legit and losing a bunch of money. 

What types of poor-quality data could affect this model?
Missing data like ip addresses or device type and location. Also, old datasets can be a problem becasue scammers always change their tactics so an AI model learning from the old datasets can miss fraud because they wouldn't be able to detect the new tacticstitanic.

What could cause this model to overfit?
A couple things actually can cause it to overfit. One thing would be it memorizing the accounts and not the clues and tactics the scammers actually use. Another thing is if the data it uses is old, it won't be able to detect the new tactics people use for fraud.

What would make you trust—or not trust—the model's predictions?
I can trust the model if it has a high accuracy percentage, and if it explains why it detected a fraud transaction. I cannot trust the model if it keeps detecting legit transactions as fraud, or if it doesn't detect the new ways people use to scam.
